From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 May 2026 00:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PlatformObjectRegistry: tolerate duplicate registrations

In the systemlib build, both libtensorflow_framework.so and
libtensorflow_cc.so embed the same stream_executor registrar TUs (e.g.
repeat_buffer_kernel_cuda.cc), so each .so's module initializer reaches the
single PlatformObjectRegistry and the registrar macros abort
`import tensorflow` on the ALREADY_EXISTS from the second insert. At the
RegisterObject insert chokepoint, report success for an identical-key
duplicate (keeping the first) rather than AlreadyExistsError; mirrors the
OpRegistry duplicate-tolerance patch.
---
--- a/third_party/xla/xla/stream_executor/platform/platform_object_registry.cc
+++ b/third_party/xla/xla/stream_executor/platform/platform_object_registry.cc
@@ -70,9 +70,18 @@
   const auto [it, inserted] = objects_.insert(std::make_pair(
       std::make_tuple(std::type_index(type), platform_id), std::move(object)));
   if (!inserted) {
-    return absl::AlreadyExistsError(absl::StrFormat(
-        "Object for trait %s and platform %s is already registered.",
-        type.name(), GetPlatformName(platform_id)));
+    // conda-forge: TensorFlow is split across several shared objects
+    // (libtensorflow_framework.so, libtensorflow_cc.so,
+    // _pywrap_tensorflow_internal.so). Each .so that contains a
+    // STREAM_EXECUTOR_REGISTER_OBJECT_STATICALLY / GPU_KERNEL_REGISTRY_
+    // REGISTER_KERNEL_STATICALLY registrar runs that registrar's module
+    // initializer once when it is loaded into the process, so the second
+    // and later registrations of the same (trait, platform) reach this
+    // singleton registry. Registered objects are immutable and these
+    // duplicates come from the same statically-compiled registrar, so they
+    // are identical; keep the first and treat the duplicate as success
+    // rather than aborting via the registrar macro's LOG(FATAL).
+    return absl::OkStatus();
   }
   return absl::OkStatus();
 }
